[6] Minnesota's Controversial Confederate Relic: The 28th Virginia Battle Flag

[7] Tucked away in a drawer at the [Minnesota Historical Society](https://mnhs.gitlab.io/archive/education-resources/www.mnhs.org/education/resources/28th-virginia-battle-flag.html) lies an obscure yet controversial artifact - a Confederate battle flag captured by a Minnesota regiment during the decisive Battle of Gettysburg.

[8] This relic, the 28th Virginia Infantry flag, has been at the center of an ongoing dispute about whether it rightfully belongs to Minnesota or should be returned to its native Virginia.

[9] Spoils of War

[10] The flag was seized in 1863 by the courageous 1st Minnesota Infantry, who suffered immense casualties staving off Confederate forces at Gettysburg.

[11] Private Marshall Sherman retrieved the dropped banner, earning himself a Medal of Honor.

[12] After the war, the flag passed from federal to state hands, exhibited periodically at Minnesota's capitol.

[13] It became part of the Historical Society's permanent collection in 1896.

[14] The Controversy

[15] But since 1905, Virginia has argued that Confederate flags should be repatriated to their states of origin.

[16] Requests for the 28th Virginia flag's return have been made in 1961, 1998, 2000, and 2013, only to be refused each time by Minnesota governors and state officials.

[17] Symbolizes Sacrifice

[18] To Minnesotans, the flag symbolizes a brave regiment's heroic sacrifice. Some oppose returning it on principle, or to avoid glorifying the Confederate cause.

[19] Officials also argue Minnesota has legal ownership after more than 150 years.

[20] Meanwhile, many in Virginia view reclaiming the banner as a point of state pride, honoring those who fought and died under its red silk and blue cross.

[21] While controversial as a Confederate emblem, to descendants it represents family heritage.

[22] A Relic

[23] The 28th Virginia flag remains one of the last unresolved captives of Gettysburg.

[24] While other states have returned their Confederate relics, Minnesota still guards its hard-earned memento despite protests from Virginia.

[25] The two states remain unlikely to find compromise on this living legacy of America's fractured past.

